# X++ Script to Create a Custom REST Service in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations (D365 F&O)

Below is a complete example of how to create a custom REST service in Dynamics 365 Finance and Operations using X++.

## 1. Create a Data Contract Class

First, create a data contract class to define the structure of your request/response data.

```xpp
[DataContractAttribute]
class MyCustomRestServiceDataContract
{
    str customerId;
    str customerName;
    str email;
    real creditLimit;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the CustomerId
/// </summary>
/// <returns>CustomerId</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('CustomerId')]
public str parmCustomerId(str _customerId = customerId)
{
    customerId = _customerId;
    return customerId;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the CustomerName
/// </summary>
/// <returns>CustomerName</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('CustomerName')]
public str parmCustomerName(str _customerName = customerName)
{
    customerName = _customerName;
    return customerName;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the Email
/// </summary>
/// <returns>Email</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('Email')]
public str parmEmail(str _email = email)
{
    email = _email;
    return email;
}

/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the CreditLimit
/// </summary>
/// <returns>CreditLimit</returns>
[DataMemberAttribute('CreditLimit')]
public real parmCreditLimit(real _creditLimit = creditLimit)
{
    creditLimit = _creditLimit;
    return creditLimit;
}
```

## 2. Create the Service Class

Next, create the service class that will contain your business logic.

```xpp
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
class MyCustomRestService
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Gets customer information by customer account
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_customerId">Customer account</param>
    /// <returns>Customer data contract</returns>
    public MyCustomRestServiceDataContract getCustomerInfo(str _customerId)
    {
        MyCustomRestServiceDataContract dataContract = new MyCustomRestServiceDataContract();
        CustTable custTable;

        try
        {
            ttsBegin;

            select firstOnly custTable
                where custTable.AccountNum == _customerId;

            if (custTable)
            {
                dataContract.parmCustomerId(custTable.AccountNum);
                dataContract.parmCustomerName(custTable.name());
                dataContract.parmEmail(custTable.email());
                dataContract.parmCreditLimit(custTable.CreditMax);
            }
            else
            {
                throw error(strFmt("Customer %1 not found", _customerId));
            }

            ttsCommit;
        }
        catch (Exception::Error)
        {
            ttsAbort;
            throw error(AifUtil::getClrErrorMessage());
        }

        return dataContract;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Updates customer information
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_dataContract">Customer data contract</param>
    /// <returns>Success status</returns>
    public boolean updateCustomerInfo(MyCustomRestServiceDataContract _dataContract)
    {
        boolean ret = false;
        CustTable custTable;

        try
        {
            ttsBegin;

            select forUpdate custTable
                where custTable.AccountNum == _dataContract.parmCustomerId();

            if (custTable)
            {
                custTable.Name = _dataContract.parmCustomerName();
                custTable.email = _dataContract.parmEmail();
                custTable.CreditMax = _dataContract.parmCreditLimit();
                custTable.update();

                ret = true;
            }
            else
            {
                throw error(strFmt("Customer %1 not found", _dataContract.parmCustomerId()));
            }

            ttsCommit;
        }
        catch (Exception::Error)
        {
            ttsAbort;
            throw error(AifUtil::getClrErrorMessage());
        }

        return ret;
    }
}
```

## 3. Create the Service Group and Service

Now, create the service group and service in the AOT:

1. In Visual Studio, right-click on the project and select **Add > New Item**
2. Select **Service Group** and name it "MyCustomRestServiceGroup"
3. Right-click on the service group and select **Add > Service**
4. Name the service "MyCustomRestService"
5. Set the **Class** property of the service to "MyCustomRestService"

## 4. Create the Service Operations

Add operations to your service:

1. Right-click on the service and select **Add > Service Operation**
2. For the first operation:
   - Name: "getCustomerInfo"
   - Return Type: "MyCustomRestServiceDataContract"
   - Parameters: Add a parameter named "_customerId" of type "str"
3. For the second operation:
   - Name: "updateCustomerInfo"
   - Return Type: "boolean"
   - Parameters: Add a parameter named "_dataContract" of type "MyCustomRestServiceDataContract"

## 5. Deploy the Service

1. Build your project
2. Right-click on the service group and select **Deploy**
3. After deployment, go to **System administration > Setup > Services and Application Integration Framework > Inbound ports**
4. Create a new inbound port:
   - Name: "MyCustomRestServicePort"
   - Description: "Custom REST service for customer operations"
   - Adapter: "REST"
   - URI: "MyCustomRestService"
   - Service: "MyCustomRestServiceGroup"
5. Activate the port

## 6. Test the Service

You can test the service using a tool like Postman:

### GET Request Example:
```
GET https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/MyCustomRestServiceGroup/MyCustomRestService/getCustomerInfo?customerId=US-001
Headers:
  Authorization: Bearer {your-access-token}
  Content-Type: application/json
```

### POST Request Example:
```
POST https://yourenvironment.cloudax.dynamics.com/api/services/MyCustomRestServiceGroup/MyCustomRestService/updateCustomerInfo
Headers:
  Authorization: Bearer {your-access-token}
  Content-Type: application/json
Body:
{
    "CustomerId": "US-001",
    "CustomerName": "Updated Customer Name",
    "Email": "updated@customer.com",
    "CreditLimit": 10000
}
```

## Additional Notes

1. **Authentication**: You'll need to authenticate using OAuth 2.0. Register an application in Azure AD and use the client credentials flow to get an access token.

2. **Error Handling**: The example includes basic error handling. You may want to enhance it based on your requirements.

3. **Security**: Ensure you implement proper security measures, including:
   - Data validation
   - Proper authorization checks
   - Input sanitization

4. **Performance**: For high-volume services, consider:
   - Adding caching
   - Optimizing database queries
   - Implementing batch operations

5. **Documentation**: Consider adding Swagger/OpenAPI documentation to your service for better discoverability.

